   "CHARACTER SHEET - HareWinter   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS DRAWN IN THE SAME MEDIUM AS THE BOOK. Warm brown kraft paper #AD8B60 with a\n  visible fibre tooth, graphite, and opaque white gouache on a dry brush. The white must show\n  brown grain through the middle of every stroke, the contour must be left open in places, and\n  the feet must be pressed dark. Do NOT render this animal smoothly just because there is no\n  background behind it. There is no cold wash on this sheet.\n\nFACE: a blunt short muzzle with the nose set high, and a jaw that is always slightly lifted -\n  a small smugness built into the anatomy, so the first page needs no acting to read as\n  \"of course I know\". Eye = a dark almond #2A241D with a SEPARATE drawn eyebrow stroke above,\n  set high on the side of the head. No dot eye, no blush, no highlight dot, no catchlight.\nFUR: the whole body is OPAQUE WHITE #F4F1E8 laid in short dry-brush strokes following the\n  growth direction, brown paper showing between the strokes so the coat is never a flat cut-out.\n  Two or three graphite strokes only, at the jaw and behind the foreleg, to separate face\n  from body in value.\n🔴 SIGNATURE DETAIL 1 - THE EAR TIPS: the last third of each ear is PURE BLACK #14100C. This is\n  the only pure black in the entire book and the whole last page depends on it. Draw the two\n  tips as two clean solid marks with a hard edge, the same size and shape as each other.\n🔴 SIGNATURE DETAIL 2 - THE LEFTOVER SUMMER: an irregular patch of bare brown paper (no white\n  paint at all) on the OUTSIDE of each hind leg, like a stain the coat has not finished\n  covering. It is there on every page and it never shrinks. It is the joke of the book - the\n  animal everyone treats as a calendar is not even finished changing.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a mountain hare, on all fours, low and heavy in the hindquarters, about\n  two and a half heads tall at the shoulder. HIND LEGS VERY LONG AND FOLDED, so even sitting\n  still it looks ready to leave. Ears long, held straight up by default and laid flat along\n  the back when pressed to the ground. Tail tiny.\n  Distinguishing point in silhouette: the two black ear tips plus the long folded hind legs.\nFEET: large hind feet with wide toes, pressed the darkest of anything on the body in graphite.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full-body side view sitting with ears up / three-quarter view pressed flat to\n  the ground with ears laid along the back / rear view showing the brown leftover patches on\n  both hind legs / a large detail of one ear showing where the pure black begins /\n  four expression close-ups: smug (chin lifted, brow level, eye half lidded),\n  uneasy (brow drawn together, eye rolled up, ears splayed apart),\n  resigned (eye half shut, brow flat, ears half down),\n  wonder (eye wide and round, brow high, pupils rolled DOWN toward its own nose).\n  Plain brown kraft background, no scenery, no cold wash.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): on this sheet the face must read apart from the body in VALUE, not\n  by adding a colour - a tonal step at the jaw, bare paper at the cheek, graphite around the\n  eye. Test: shrink this sheet to thumbnail size - the expression must still be legible.\nSCENE token: HareWinter.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ForestNeighbours   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - brown kraft paper, graphite only.\n🔴 NOT ONE STROKE OF WHITE PAINT ON ANY OF THESE FOUR. This is the rule that makes the hare\n  visible from far away, and it is broken the moment one of them gets a white belly or a white\n  rump. They are made of the same brown paper as the forest; the hare is not.\n  Each of them is defined by SILHOUETTE first, because most of the time only a head, a back or\n  a shape in a hollow will be showing.\n\nSQUIRREL - a red squirrel drawn in graphite: small, upright, LONG WINTER EAR TUFTS standing\n  straight up (that is the whole silhouette cue), cheeks bulging, tail carried in an S over\n  the back. Claws drawn as three separate hooks so it can grip bark vertically.\nGEESE - one goose shape repeated: neck stretched straight forward, wings at the same beat, body\n  a blunt wedge. 🔴 Drawn as a ROW, never as a countable tally - the reader must feel a line\n  leaving, not count twelve birds. One straggler at the back has its head turned sideways.\nROE DEER - slim, high at the shoulder, thick winter coat standing up along the neck like a\n  short mane, no antlers, big rounded ears, black nose, legs thin as sticks. Drawn with fewer\n  strokes than any other animal here.\nBROWN BEAR - one enormous low mass, about four hare-lengths long, small round ears set wide,\n  heavy shoulder hump, small eye almost lost in the head. Heavy eyelids. Seen mostly as a back\n  and a shoulder blocking a hole.\nREFERENCE SHEET: all four side by side at correct relative scale, next to a small HareWinter\n  silhouette for size. Also show each one as it appears in the book: the squirrel clinging\n  vertically to bark / one goose in flight / the deer's back and neckline seen from the side /\n  the bear's back half inside a den mouth. Plain brown kraft background.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): every face here must read apart from its body in VALUE using\n  graphite tone and bare paper only. Eyes, brow and mouth must never sink into one flat mass.\nSCENE tokens: Squirrel, GeeseFlock, RoeDeer, BrownBear. Never write \"Hare\" alone for the\n  hero - always HareWinter."
