   "CHARACTER SHEET - LittleLamb   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS PRINTED IN THE SAME PROCESS AS THE BOOK. Hand-pulled stencil / flat-plate ink on\n  cool white paper #F3F3EF. Flat planes only - no brush, no pencil line, no gradient. Ink slightly\n  mottled inside each plane, one or two starved spots, neighbouring plates a hair out of register.\n  Torn stencil edges. Do NOT render this lamb smoothly just because there is no background behind\n  her. There is NO fog plate anywhere on this sheet - she is always FOG 0.\n\nFLEECE: 🔴 BARE UNPRINTED PAPER #F3F3EF. Her body is a hole in the ink, not a painted white shape.\n  One slate #4E5A5E contour runs round it, broken in two or three places where the plate went out\n  of register. FIVE OR SIX short curved stencil nicks along the back and rump say \"wool\" and that\n  is all the texture that exists. NOT fluffy, NOT fibrous, 🔴 NOT felted wool, NOT fabric.\nFACE: one darker slate plane #4E5A5E for the whole face and muzzle, clearly darker than the fleece.\n  🔴 EYES ARE HOLES - two small unprinted paper ovals left inside that dark plane, with a tiny\n  #23282A dot inside each. A CLOSED eye = no hole at all, replaced by one short printed slate\n  curve. Both versions must be on this sheet. One short mouth line. No blush, no lashes, no\n  catchlight.\nEARS: two flat ear planes, slate outside and a paler inner shape. 🔴 THE EAR ANGLE IS HALF THE\n  PERFORMANCE - draw them large enough to read at thumbnail size and always draw both.\nLEGS: four thin slate legs, each one flat tapered plane, with small dark hoof shapes. 🔴 SHE IS\n  ALWAYS ON FOUR LEGS. She never stands up, never gestures, never holds anything.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a young lamb - short body, high square-ish stance, head large for the body,\n  a stubby tail. Silhouette = bright paper body + dark head + two upright ears, readable at\n  thumbnail size and instantly distinct from the adult ewes (which are longer, lower and heavier).\n🔴 NO BLUE ANYWHERE ON HER. The blue belongs to her mother's back alone. No collar, no bell.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body from slightly above (the book's main angle) / three-quarter view walking\n  with one foreleg reaching / rear view seen from behind with both ears forward / head close-up;\n  plus FIVE ear-and-eye expression close-ups:\n    grazing-content (head down, ears loose and sideways, eyes half),\n    calling-out (head up, mouth open wide, eyes wide holes, ears splayed different ways),\n    creeping-forward (head low, both ears bolt forward and parallel, body crouched),\n    hiding-afraid (both ears flat back, one eye showing past an edge, body pressed low),\n    🔴 EYES CLOSED-LISTENING (no eye holes at all, one short curve each, both ears up level and\n      aimed the same way, muzzle slightly raised, mouth line easy) - THIS IS THE LAST PAGE.\n  Plain cool white paper background, no scenery, no fog, no blue.\nSCENE token: LittleLamb.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - MotherEwe   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME PROCESS AS ABOVE - flat stencil planes, torn edges, no brush, no gradient, cool white\n  paper. She uses the SAME face grammar as LittleLamb (dark face plane, eyes as unprinted holes,\n  flat angled ears) so that they read as the same species drawn by the same hand.\n\nBUILD: an adult ewe - longer, lower and heavier than the lamb, deeper chest, straighter back,\n  a broader flatter head. Fleece is the same bare unprinted paper, with the slate contour heavier\n  and a few more stencil nicks along the flank.\n🔴 THE BLUE MARK: one flat, opaque, hard-edged shepherd's-blue #2551B8 plane on her BACK, over the\n  shoulders - the size of a hand, an irregular smear with a torn edge, exactly the shape a farmer\n  sprays. It is the ONLY saturated colour in the whole book. It is never on any other sheep, it\n  never moves, it never changes size, and it never glows or spills onto the fleece around it.\n🔴 THE BELL: a slate collar strap round her neck with one small BARE UNPRINTED PAPER shape hanging\n  from it - that hole of paper IS the brass. No metallic gradient, no shine, no yellow ink.\n  The strap hangs and the bell swings to one side when she walks.\n🔴 SILHOUETTE MUST BE THE ONE THAT DIFFERS: among seven grey ewes she is the only one with a collar\n  strap and a mark on her back. That is the whole plot. Both must be visible from behind and from\n  three-quarter rear, because the reader mostly sees her walking away.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body side-on grazing with the head down / three-quarter REAR view walking away\n  showing the blue mark and the swinging bell (🔴 the book's most repeated view) / 🔴 STOPPED AND\n  TURNED - body still facing uphill but head and neck twisted back over the shoulder, mouth open\n  calling, both ears forward (this is the p9 pose and it must be unmistakable in silhouette) /\n  head lowered to nuzzle a lamb-height point.\n  Plain cool white paper background, no fog, no scenery.\n\n🔴 FOG VARIANTS OF THIS EWE (put all four on the sheet, same pose, in a row):\n  MotherEwe at FOG 0 - full: white fleece, dark face, blue mark at full strength, bell bright.\n  MotherEwe at FOG 1 - one pale pull over her: shape and blue mark and bell still readable, all\n    fleece nicks and face detail gone.\n  MotherEwe at FOG 2 - 🔴 a soft pale-grey lump. No face, no legs separated, no bell, and the blue\n    reduced to a faint bluish stain that could be mistaken for a patch of fog. THIS IS THE STATE\n    THE WHOLE MISUNDERSTANDING DEPENDS ON - it must be genuinely ambiguous, not merely lighter.\n  MotherEwe at FOG 3 - nothing at all: flat pale ink where she was.\nSCENE token: MotherEwe.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - HillCast   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\n🔴 SAME PROCESS - flat stencil planes on cool white paper, torn edges, no brush, no gradient.\n\nFlockSeven (the flock): 🔴 ONE SINGLE EWE STENCIL SHAPE, PRINTED SEVEN TIMES. Same outline, same\n  size, only the position and the direction they face change. Four full-grown ewes, two half-grown\n  lambs and MotherEwe make SEVEN. 🔴 They are printed from the same shape ON PURPOSE so that a\n  child can count them and match the seven on the first page against the seven in the fog. Do not\n  individualise them, do not vary their sizes for prettiness, do not give them faces on the wide\n  pages. None of them carries a collar or a mark except MotherEwe.\n  Put them on the sheet as: the seven at FOG 0 grazing scattered / 🔴 the same seven at FOG 2 as\n  seven soft grey lumps (the p7 view) - and the two rows must obviously be the same seven shapes.\n\nHillPony (p5 only): a stocky Welsh mountain pony - short legs, thick neck, heavy jaw, a long wet\n  mane stuck flat to the forehead in three or four slate strands. Built from big flat planes:\n  one slate body plane, a darker mane plane, four blunt hoof shapes. Eyes are unprinted holes like\n  the sheep, set low and calm. 🔴 Not friendly, not frightening - the face of an animal that\n  mentions something in passing. Twice the height of the lamb. NO blue, NO bell, NO tack, NO saddle.\n  Put him on the sheet twice: whole at FOG 0, and 🔴 as the book actually uses him - head and chest\n  at FOG 1 with the hindquarters at FOG 2, so the reader sees an animal half-delivered by the fog.\n\nSCALE ROW (required): LittleLamb, one adult ewe and HillPony side by side at correct relative size,\n  with a lichened boulder and a drystone wall course for scale.\nSCENE tokens: FlockSeven, HillPony. Never write \"sheep\" or \"pony\" alone.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - FogStepChart   (bake FOURTH - 🔴 this sheet IS the style rule)\n\n🔴 THIS IS NOT A SCENE. It is a printed swatch chart that defines the fog plate for every later\n  page, and it is the single most important reference in this book. Words cannot hold this rule -\n  this image has to.\n\nPrint ONE simple hill scene at the top of the sheet: a drystone wall running across, a lichened\n  boulder, a tuft of rushes, and one ewe standing beside them, all at FOG 0 on cool white paper,\n  flat stencil planes, moss-green ground and slate stone.\n\nThen print THE SAME SCENE FOUR TIMES IN A ROW BENEATH IT, each with one more pull of the pale fog\n  ink #E8EBE6 laid over the top:\n  1. FOG 0 - untouched. Wall joints, lichen, grass, the ewe's face and legs all present.\n  2. FOG 1 - one pull. Everything steps toward pale together. Shapes and outlines still read;\n     🔴 ALL interior description is gone - no wall joints, no lichen, no grass blades, no face.\n  3. FOG 2 - two pulls. 🔴 Only outer silhouettes survive as soft pale-grey lumps. The wall is one\n     bar, the boulder is one blob, the ewe is one lump with no face, no legs and no direction.\n  4. FOG 3 - three pulls. 🔴 COMPLETELY EMPTY. Flat pale ink from edge to edge with nothing in it.\n     No ghost, no faint outline, no hint of where the wall was.\n\n🔴 REQUIRED AT THE BOTTOM OF THE SHEET: one strip showing two of these states meeting side by side\n  along a single TORN STENCIL EDGE - a hard, fibrous, ragged boundary with no fade between them.\n  This is how fog meets clear air in this book.\n\n🔴 WHAT THIS CHART IS NOT: it is not four exposures of the same photograph, not four levels of\n  blur, not four opacities of a layer, not a depth-of-field ramp. Each step is a further FLAT\n  PRINTED FILM that covers. Print, not focus.\nPlain cool white paper, no lettering or numbers on the sheet, no blue anywhere."
