   "CHARACTER SHEET - MouseSmall   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS DRAWN IN THE SAME MEDIUM AS THE BOOK. Dip-pen drawing ink and one flat gouache\n  field on warm dust paper #E6DECB. The nib wobble must be visible, contours must be left open in\n  places, and the body must be a solid warm black-brown mass. Do NOT render this animal smoothly\n  just because there is no background behind it. No red anywhere on this sheet.\n\nFACE: a small blunt muzzle with a rounded nose. Eyes are DRAWN - a dark curved shape with a\n  SEPARATE eyebrow stroke above, set wide and low, so the eye can be wide open, half lidded,\n  squeezed shut, or seen as a single eye through a gap between two forepaws. Whiskers are four\n  fine ink lines that stand STRAIGHT UP when the mouse is smelling something. No dot eyes, no\n  blush, no highlight dot, no glossy catchlight.\nBODY: one solid mass of warm black-brown ink #2A241F. 🔴 The belly, the inner ears and a small\n  patch on the muzzle are left as BARE PAPER - that is the only value separation the face has,\n  and it is what makes the expression readable when the mouse is 1/12 of the frame.\n  Ears large and round, set high, drawn as two open circles.\nFOREPAWS: small and clearly separated into three fingers-worth of shape, because 🔴 THE FOREPAWS\n  COVER THE EYES ON FIVE PAGES and the reader must see them as two distinct paws, not one blob.\n  Draw a version where the two paws are pressed shut with no gap, and a version where they are\n  parted just enough for ONE eye.\n🔴 TAIL - THE ACTING INSTRUMENT: the tail is LONGER THAN THE BODY, a single thin ink line that\n  swells at the root. It is how the reader reads the mood at thumbnail size:\n  stiff straight back = braced and pretending to be brave / wound once around the whole body =\n  frightened / lying loose and slightly curled at the side = at ease.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a small round-backed mouse about two heads tall on all fours, rising onto its\n  hind legs only when it uses its forepaws. Big ears, long tail, no waist - the silhouette is a\n  dark comma with two circles on top.\n🔴 SIZE ANCHOR: the whole mouse, standing on its hind legs, is SMALLER THAN ONE OF THE DOG'S\n  FOREPAWS. Draw this comparison on the sheet: the mouse standing beside a single dog forepaw.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body side view on all fours / standing on hind legs with both forepaws\n  pressed over both eyes, tail stiff / same pose but the paws parted for one eye / curled into a\n  ball with the face fully covered and the tail wound round / sitting with forepaws down and tail\n  loose / a close-up of the head showing the SHAKE LINE version of the contour beside the calm\n  version, so the difference is unmistakable.\n  Plain dust paper background, no scenery.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): on this sheet the face must read apart from the body in VALUE, not by\n  adding a colour - the bare-paper muzzle patch and inner ears carry it. Eyes, brow and mouth must\n  never sink into the solid body mass. Test: shrink this sheet to thumbnail size - the expression\n  must still be legible. This does NOT add a colour.\nSCENE token: MouseSmall.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - FarmDog   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - dip-pen ink line and flat gouache on warm dust paper #E6DECB.\n  🔴 THIS ANIMAL IS MOSTLY BARE PAPER. Its body is drawn with a line and left unpainted, with\n  three or four solid ink A patches. It is the biggest thing in the book and the lightest - do not\n  fill it in, and do not make it black. It turns dark ONLY when it is inside a slate shade.\n\nFACE: a broad blunt muzzle, a large solid nose, loose jowls, and long drop ears. Eyes are DRAWN -\n  a dark almond with a SEPARATE eyebrow stroke above, and the skin above the brow creases into two\n  short lines when it is worried. It must be able to: sleep (eyes shut, ears down) / snap awake\n  (whites showing all round, ears flat back) / stare past the mouse at something on the ground /\n  peer over its own forepaws with only the top half of the eyes showing / half close them, at ease.\nBODY: a big farm collie-type dog. Bare paper coat with ink A patches: one patch over the left ear\n  and around (NOT over) the left eye, one across the shoulders, one at the base of the tail.\n  🔴 No collar, no tag, no name plate, no letters anywhere - this book carries no lettering at all.\n🔴 FOREPAWS - THE SIZE ANCHOR OF THE BOOK: the forepaws are drawn deliberately large and heavy,\n  with the toes clearly separated and the claws visible, because the whole first page turns on\n  \"one of its paws is bigger than me\". ONE FOREPAW IS LARGER THAN THE ENTIRE MOUSE.\n  Draw that comparison on this sheet.\n🔴 MUZZLE - THE OTHER SIZE ANCHOR: the bridge of the nose is broad and long, and a plain empty tin\n  can (a cylinder about as tall as the mouse) laid beside it on the same ground is CLEARLY SMALLER\n  than the muzzle. Draw that comparison on this sheet too - it is the reversal of the whole book\n  and it must be settled on the sheet before any scene.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: heavy chested, thick neck, a long feathered tail. When it makes itself small\n  it folds all four legs under the body, rounds its back, and tucks the tail deep between the hind\n  legs - the silhouette becomes a closed oval, and it is still bigger than everything else in the\n  yard. That contradiction is the joke.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body side view lying on its side asleep, muzzle between the forepaws /\n  scrambling up onto its front legs, back-pedalling, ears flat, whites of the eyes showing /\n  folded into the smallest possible crouch, trembling, tail tucked - drawn WITH the shake line /\n  lying down with both forepaws brought up over its own nose, eyes half visible above them /\n  lying at ease, forepaws stretched forward, tail sweeping / a large detail of one forepaw with\n  the mouse standing beside it, and of the muzzle with the tin can lying beside it.\n  Plain dust paper background, no scenery.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): the face must read apart from the body in VALUE, not by adding a\n  colour - the pale bare-paper muzzle against the ink patches carries it. Eyes, brow and mouth must\n  never sink into a flat mass. Test: at thumbnail size the expression is still legible.\nANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: a quadruped throughout. It never stands on two legs, never has hands or\n  fingers, wears nothing. Lifting both forepaws to its own face while lying down is the maximum.\nSCENE token: FarmDog."
