    "prompt": "CAMERA: medium wide, slightly low angle - a little below the seated mouse. 🔴 THE CAMERA HAS CROSSED\n  THE YARD: it now stands in front of the straw pile on the right side, and the wall hole has\n  receded to a small opening far away at the left rear. Do not repeat the p1 framing.\n  The dog's forepaw and the mouse are at centre; the can and the stone are in the right foreground.\nSUBJECT: FarmDog lies down on the straw again, both forepaws stretched out in front side by side,\n  muzzle laid on the straw, eyes half closed, ears loose and easy. Its tail sweeps the straw from\n  side to side and the strands fan out under it.\n  MouseSmall sits in the dirt right beside one outstretched forepaw with its hind legs folded, its\n  back just touching the paw. 🔴 BOTH FOREPAWS ARE DOWN in front of it, not covering anything. Its\n  face is lifted toward the dog's face at the upper right, mouth open, speaking. The tail lies loose\n  and slightly curled at its side.\nSETTING: the yard map from this new side - wall base and the small hole far away at the left rear,\n  bucket at the left rear, butt at the right rear, flat stone in the right foreground. The straw is\n  freshly pressed again under the dog.\nPROPS: 🔴 the empty can is still wedged beside the flat stone AT EXACTLY THE ANGLE IT HAD ON p10 -\n  lower end buried, upper end riding on the stone, the earth mound still in front of it. Nobody has\n  tidied it away. ⚠️ On this page the can is in the near foreground and the dog's muzzle is behind,\n  so they are at different depths - do NOT invite a size comparison between them here.\nMARKS: every mark of the morning is still in the dirt - ① the can's thin dry track, ② the claw\n  scrape from straw to butt, ③ the mouse's footprints from the hole, ④ the shove-track and mound.\n  🔴 Mark ⑤ is created here and laid on top of them all: a line of big fresh dog pawprints coming\n  back from the butt to the straw.\nSHAKE LINE: 🔴 on MouseSmall only, and very faint - one light second contour, no ticks, nowhere near\n  the strength of p7. The fear has not gone; it has got smaller. Nothing else shakes.\nSHADOW: 🔴 THE SUN HAS RISEN AND THE SHADOWS ARE SHORT. Every slate shape on this page is a fraction\n  of the length it had on p1-p10, and the flat contrast of the dry yard has softened. This shortening\n  is the only thing that says the morning has passed - never lengthen them again.\nSIZE CHAIN: 🔴 THE OTHER MEASURING PAGE. One outstretched forepaw is larger than the whole seated\n  mouse, and they are touching, on the same ground, in the same light - so the difference is settled\n  at a glance and needs no explanation.\nGRADE: quadrupeds as in the anchor. The mouse sits on its haunches; the dog lies down. No hands.\nFINISH: the dog, the mouse and the five marks finished. The straw directly under the dog and the\n  wedged can with its stone half-finished. The wall and its little far hole, the bucket, the butt\n  and the field are rough unfinished pen sketch.\nTONE: 🔴 the dog's body and the mouse's outline run together on the straw so that the line between\n  them almost disappears - the two of them read as one shape. The eye goes first to the pair sitting\n  together, then to the wedge of shade under the can in the right foreground, then out to the tiny\n  hole in the wall far away at the left; the distance travelled this morning lies between those two\n  points. Warm, dry, and still a little careful.\nNO LETTERING anywhere in this image. No collar, no tag on the dog."
