   "CHARACTER SHEET - BadgerCobbler   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS MADE IN THE SAME WAY AS THE BOOK. Flat printed brown areas on a honey-cream\n  board (one, two or three passes), with a small number of FINE DRAWN LINES on top. 🔴 THE BODY\n  IS FLAT-FILLED WITH NO MODELLING. Do not render this badger with soft fur shading just because\n  there is no background behind it. There is NO BLACK on this sheet and no red.\n\nFACE: 🔴 a badger's face kept as a badger's - a pale muzzle with the two dark stripes running\n  from the nose up over the eyes and back past the ears. 🔴 The stripes are printed in the\n  THREE-PASS brown #6E4A2C, never in black. On the face:\n    - ONE small dark almond eye per side, sitting inside the stripe but separated from it by a\n      thin reserve of the pale muzzle so the eye still reads\n    - ONE SEPARATE BROW STROKE above each eye - set low and level. 🔴 This badger's face barely\n      changes for thirteen pages: the brows stay level, the mouth stays a soft line. It is a\n      calm, loose, unhurried old face. 🔴 NEVER a knowing smile, never a wink, never a look that\n      says \"I planned this\" - the last pages depend on him not making a show of anything.\n    - a small round nose, a thin mouth line, three whisker strokes per side\n  Old age is stated by 🔴 heavy loose eyelids drawn as one extra line, and a slightly sunken\n  cheek - not by wrinkles all over.\nBODY: pale grey-cream fur as bare board with a one-pass area over the back and haunches; the\n  striped head; 🔴 a TWO-PASS LEATHER APRON #B58455 with a neck strap, one chest pocket and its\n  lower corners rubbed round, its edge stated by a fine broken line. Short thick limbs, broad\n  low shoulders, a heavy round back that stoops.\n🔴 ANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: upright on two legs, front paws work as hands, five short thick\n  digits per hand drawn with fine lines. He never walks on four legs.\n🔴 THE HANDS ARE THE MOST IMPORTANT THING ON THIS SHEET. Bake them large and separately, because\n  they carry twelve of the thirteen pages. Six hand states, each drawn big:\n  1. MEASURING - one hand looping a fine thread round a foot, the other holding a stub of pencil\n  2. CARVING - both hands on a curved knife and a block, the knife pulled at a slant\n  3. CUTTING - one hand pressing a paper pattern flat onto leather, the other drawing a curved\n     knife toward the body in a straight pull\n  4. 🔴 SADDLE STITCHING - two needles on ONE thread, the left needle going away from the body\n     and the right needle coming toward it 🔴 THROUGH THE SAME HOLE, so both arms are thrown wide\n     apart. Show the thread crossing as an X between them and a wax lump on the thread\n  5. LASTING - one hand hauling the damp leather edge down with lasting pincers using body\n     weight, the other tapping a small tack with a light hammer, two spare tacks held in the lips\n  6. 🔴 HOLDING STILL - one hand simply steadying a shoe, no tool, not touching the thread. This\n     is the last page's hand and it must look like deliberate restraint, not like helping\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: an old, low, heavy badger, about four and a half heads tall. Silhouette =\n  stooped round back + wide apron + short thick limbs, instantly distinct from the rabbit.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full body standing at the bench / seated on a low stool with a block gripped\n  between the knees / standing and leaning weight into the pincers / seated with a shoe gripped\n  between both knees, elbows thrown wide / seen from behind, working on a different shoe (the\n  page where he does not look up); plus the six hand states above; plus THREE expression\n  close-ups, all nearly the same: neutral working / looking up without changing anything /\n  🔴 the very slight lift at one corner of the mouth used exactly once.\n  Plain honey-cream board background, no scenery, no red, no black.\nSCENE token: BadgerCobbler.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - RabbitKit   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE - flat printed areas plus fine drawn lines, no modelling, no black, no\n  red. 🔴 THIS IS THE CHARACTER THAT ACTS. The badger's face barely moves for thirteen pages, so\n  every emotional beat of the book is on this sheet.\n\nFACE: a young rabbit, warm pale grey-cream as bare board with a one-pass area over the back.\n    - ONE small dark almond eye per side, set wide\n    - ONE SEPARATE BROW STROKE above each - 🔴 this stroke is the whole emotional range\n    - a small nose, a split upper lip drawn with two short lines, three whisker strokes per side\n  🔴 NOT dot-eyes. No blush, no highlight dot, no glossy catchlight, no pink inner ear.\n🔴 THE EARS ARE THE SECOND ACTING ORGAN. Bake them in five states as separate small silhouettes:\n  both up straight and still / both flopped forward over the face (bored, waiting) / one swung\n  round toward a sound at the window / both laid flat back (startled) / 🔴 both bolt upright and\n  rigid (the moment the needle is held out).\nBODY: 🔴 a LOOSE BAGGY JUMPER, one flat two-pass area with the ribbing at the cuffs and hem\n  stated by two or three fine lines each, the sleeves too long so they swallow the wrists.\n  Nothing else worn - 🔴 THE FEET ARE BARE THROUGH THE WHOLE BOOK UNTIL THE LAST PAGE. That is\n  the point of the book.\n🔴 ANTHROPOMORPHISM GRADE: upright on two legs, front paws work as hands, four slim digits per\n  hand drawn with fine lines. Never on four legs.\n🔴 THE BARE HIND FOOT IS A PROP AND MUST BE BAKED LARGE: seen from above, flat on a surface, the\n  toes slightly curled, the pad and four toes clear enough that a drawn outline round it can be\n  compared with it. Bake it TWICE - 🔴 ONCE AT THE START SIZE and 🔴 ONCE ONE THUMB-WIDTH LONGER\n  (the grown foot). The difference must be small but unmistakable when the two are set side by\n  side, because the whole span of the book is measured by it.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a small young rabbit, about three and a half heads tall, thin limbs, a\n  round head made rounder by the baggy jumper. Silhouette = long ears + baggy jumper + bare\n  feet.\nREFERENCE SHEET: standing in a doorway looking up with the head thrown right back / sitting up\n  on a workbench with one bare hind foot laid out flat in front / elbows on the bench edge with\n  the chin in both hands and the ears flopped forward / crouched under a bench looking up /\n  sitting on the floor with the knees hugged / 🔴 FROZEN - sitting, both hands stopped in front\n  of the chest, ears bolt upright / 🔴 BOTH HANDS ALONE, ONE GRIPPING A NEEDLE AWKWARDLY (the\n  knuckles drawn tight) AND THE OTHER WINDING A THREAD ROUND ITSELF AND PULLING SIDEWAYS - drawn\n  large, since one whole page is nothing but these two hands / walking away from us over a\n  threshold, one heel lifted; plus FIVE expression close-ups: hopeful-asking (brows up, ears up,\n  mouth just open), bored-waiting (brows flat, ears flopped forward, eyes following something),\n  🔴 puzzled at its own foot (brows pushed together, head down, mouth small and open), stopped\n  and disbelieving (eyes at their widest, brows high, ears rigid), 🔴 concentrating hard (brows\n  driven down, mouth pressed shut, one ear slightly out of line).\n  Plain honey-cream board background, no scenery, no red, no black.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): the eye and brow must read apart from the head in VALUE - a thin\n  paler reserve where they sit. Test: shrink this sheet to thumbnail size and the five moods must\n  still be distinguishable from the ears alone.\nSCENE token: RabbitKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ShelfStages   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3 — 🔴 THE MOST IMPORTANT SHEET IN THIS BOOK)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE. This sheet is the spine of the book: the shoe grows through SIX states\n  and the reader is meant to see, on every spread, exactly which state it has reached. If the six\n  are not locked here, thirteen pages will not agree with each other.\n  Bake all six IN A ROW, left to right, all at the same size, all from the same three-quarter\n  angle, on plain honey-cream board. Each one is a flat printed area plus fine drawn lines.\n\n  1. 🔴 THE WOODEN LAST - a block carved into the shape of a foot, three-pass brown #6E4A2C, its\n     tool marks four or five short fine lines running with the form. A round wedge in its top for\n     pulling it out later.\n  2. 🔴 FIVE CUT LEATHER PIECES - flat two-pass brown #B58455 shapes laid out neatly: a vamp\n     (front), two quarters (sides/back), a tongue, and one lining piece. Each has a fine broken\n     line along its cut edge. 🔴 Beside them, THE HIDE THEY CAME OUT OF, with the five shapes\n     missing from it as holes - so the reader can see it was one hide.\n  3. 🔴 THE SEWN UPPER - the pieces joined into the top half of a shoe, still soft and slumped\n     because there is nothing inside it yet, with 🔴 A ROW OF FINE RED DASHES along each seam.\n  4. 🔴 THE UPPER PULLED OVER THE LAST - the same upper now stretched tight over the wooden last,\n     printed ONE PASS DARKER than state 3 because the leather is damp, with the leather folded\n     under the bottom and 🔴 A RING OF SMALL DRAWN TACK HEADS holding it, countable.\n  5. 🔴 THE SAME, DRIED AND WITH THE SOLE ON - the tacks gone, the leather printed back to its\n     normal step because it has dried, and a thick stiff sole #B98B4A laid against the bottom\n     with 🔴 A RING OF FINE RED STITCHES round its edge. Show it once HALF ROUND and once\n     ALMOST ALL ROUND WITH ONE GAP LEFT, and in that gap 🔴 TWO SMALL DRAWN AWL HOLES.\n  6. 🔴 THE FINISHED SHOE, LAST REMOVED - the same shoe standing with its mouth open toward us,\n     🔴 THE INSIDE A FLAT THREE-PASS BROWN AREA (the darkest thing in the book, and it is brown,\n     not black), the red stitching closed in one complete ring round the sole, and 🔴 ONE STITCH\n     IN THAT RING VISIBLY SLIGHTLY CROOKED. Beside it, the wooden last, out.\n🔴 The six must be distinguishable AT THUMBNAIL SIZE and in this order. Test: cover the labels,\n  shuffle them, and they should still be sortable by anyone.\nSCENE tokens: LastBlock, CutPieces, SewnUpper, LastedShoe, SoledShoe, FinishedShoe.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - WorkshopKit   (bake FOURTH, attach as @image4)\n\n🔴 SAME MEDIUM AS ABOVE. These are the props that carry the density of the book, so they are\n  settled here and never re-invented in a scene.\n\nTOOL WALL (🔴 this is the decorative band of the book): on a hanging rail, drawn with fine lines\n  only - TWO AWLS with round wooden handles, ONE CURVED KNIFE (a half-moon blade), LASTING\n  PINCERS with a hammer face on one jaw, a small LIGHT HAMMER, and 🔴 FIVE OR SIX WOODEN LASTS IN\n  DIFFERENT SIZES hanging in a row from largest to smallest. 🔴 The row of lasts is the closest\n  thing this book has to an ornament and it repeats on many pages - fix its order here.\n🔴 RED WAXED THREAD: a SKEIN on a nail, drawn as a loose figure-of-eight of fine red lines. Plus\n  a single loose strand, and a small waxed lump on the end of a strand. 🔴 The thread is drawn,\n  never real - no fibre, no fuzz, no twist detail.\n🔴 THE PAPER PATTERN (the plant of the book, appears on three pages): one sheet of pale board-\n  coloured paper with 🔴 THE OUTLINE OF A RABBIT'S HIND FOOT drawn on it in a fine brown line,\n  and 🔴 A CLEARLY VISIBLE EMPTY GAP OF ONE THUMB-WIDTH BETWEEN THE DRAWN TOE-LINE AND THE TOES\n  OF THE OUTLINE. Bake it THREE times: FRESH AND FLAT / CREASED, WITH A BARE FOOT ON IT AND THE\n  TOES OVERSHOOTING THE LINE / FOLDED UP AND SET ASIDE. 🔴 The size of that gap is the most\n  important measurement in the book - it is decided on this sheet.\nTHE HANGING HIDE: one whole uncut sheet of two-pass leather on a wall hook, the largest flattest\n  object in the workshop. Bake it twice: WHOLE, and 🔴 WITH FIVE SHAPES CUT OUT OF IT.\nWOOD SHAVINGS: a dozen individual shavings, each TWO FINE CURVED LINES, plus a small heap of\n  them. Never a texture.\nFURNITURE: the low workbench with its shallow drawers (one pulled open), the shelf, the low\n  stool, a shallow water bowl and a damp rag, a stub of pencil, a small brazier with three fine\n  lines rising, 🔴 AND THE BIG HAND-ME-DOWN SHOES - a pair of worn shoes clearly too large, with\n  their backs collapsed, so that one of them slipping off a heel is legible.\nTHE WINDOW: the opening, its sill, and 🔴 SIX SEASON PLATES for what is behind it, all at the\n  same size so they can be swapped: yellow leaves and burrs / bare branches on grey / first snow\n  along the branch tops / falling snow with a chimney and one line of smoke / 🔴 WHITEOUT (the\n  brightest, palest, emptiest one - the lane is gone) / spring green new leaves with a wet\n  cobbled lane. 🔴 The tree is the season colour plus a few fine branch lines - NOT drawn like a\n  botanical plate.\n  Plus 🔴 THE FLOOR PATCH: the clean-edged printed patch of the season's colour that the window\n  throws onto the floor. One shape, six colours. NOT a glow, NOT a shaft, NOT a gradient.\n  Plain honey-cream board background, no black.\nSCENE tokens: ToolWall, RedSkein, PaperPattern, WholeHide, BigOldShoes, SeasonWindow, FloorPatch."
